## 1. Scope and definitions

“Licensor” means Gerald Höhn or another copyright holder who expressly applies this
license to material that they have authority to license.

“Work” means the ATLAS source code, formal definitions, proof scripts, proof terms,
certificates, data and documentation expressly designated as covered by this license.
“Modified Work” includes modifications and adaptations of that covered material.
Third-party material and dependencies remain subject to their own licenses.

“You” means the person or legal entity exercising the permissions granted here.

“Commercial Use” means use primarily intended for or directed toward commercial
advantage or monetary compensation, including incorporation into a commercial
product or service and internal use for developing or operating such products or
services. Use by or on behalf of a for-profit entity requires separate permission.
Academic publication in a commercial journal, ordinary academic employment and
research cost reimbursement do not, by themselves, make otherwise noncommercial
research commercial. Research performed for a commercial client's product or service
is not covered by that exception.

“AI Training Use” means using the Work or Modified Work, or data derived from them,
to train, pretrain, fine-tune, distill, or otherwise optimize the learned parameters
or learned behavior of a machine-learning or artificial-intelligence system. It
includes use as training examples, target outputs, rewards, feedback or synthetic
training-data inputs, and creation or distribution of datasets intended for these
purposes. It includes training during inference or deployment.

## 2. Conditional research permission

Subject to these terms, the Licensor grants You a worldwide, royalty-free,
nonexclusive copyright license to access, reproduce, run, verify, study, modify and
redistribute the Work and Modified Work for noncommercial research, education and
personal study. No ownership is transferred.

This grant does not authorize Commercial Use or AI Training Use. Either requires
prior, separate written permission from the Licensor. The AI Training Use restriction
applies whether the activity is commercial or noncommercial and whether undertaken
by a company, university, nonprofit organization or individual.

Noncommercial evaluation of a fixed AI system without training or optimization is
permitted under these terms. Evaluation used to select or optimize a model for
commercial development requires separate permission.

A researcher may use an AI assistant to inspect or help verify the Work for a
permitted research purpose, provided the Work is not supplied under terms authorizing
the provider to use it for training or other prohibited purposes. This exception
does not grant the provider permission to train on the Work.

## 3. Attribution and scholarly citation

You must preserve applicable copyright, authorship, attribution and license notices.
When distributing the Work or Modified Work, You must provide a copy of this license,
identify ATLAS and Gerald Höhn, and prominently identify changes You have made.
Redistribution of covered material and its modifications must remain subject to
these conditions; You may not purport to grant permissions that this license withholds.
Every distributed derivative work containing or adapting material covered by this
license must include the complete text of this license and retain its attribution,
commercial-use, AI-training and redistribution conditions for the covered material
and its adaptations. This requirement applies to source and compiled distributions;
for compiled distributions, include the license in the accompanying documentation
or other readily accessible notices. Independent, separable works and third-party
material are not automatically relicensed by this requirement.

If You publish a scholarly work reporting results obtained through material use of
the Work, including its formal constructions or machine-checked proofs, You must
acknowledge ATLAS and Gerald Höhn and include an appropriate bibliographic citation
to the ATLAS research note identified in the citation information accompanying the
version used. Identify the software version or commit where reasonably practicable.
If no research-note citation is supplied, cite Gerald Höhn, the ATLAS project title,
the repository URL and the version used. A reference-list entry is sufficient;
coauthorship and endorsement are not required or implied.

“Material use” means a substantive contribution to the reported construction,
proof, computation or verification, rather than incidental consultation. Mere
mention, independent discovery, criticism or review does not trigger this clause.
An inadvertent citation omission may be corrected through an erratum, an updated
preprint, or another reasonably accessible correction associated with the publication.

